'''[[Sadakazu Tanizaki]]''' (谷崎 貞益, ''Tanizaki Sadakazu'', born September 27, 1961) is an Okaiken politician who served as the 54th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for a brief period in 2016. He also served as president of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) for two non-consecutive terms (2016 and 2019–2021).

==Early life and education==
[[Sadakazu Tanizaki]] was born on September 27, 1961, in Okami, Okaiken. After completing his secondary education, he pursued advanced studies at the prestigious [[Hartford School of Economics]], where he focused on fiscal policy and international trade. His academic background and expertise in finance would later define his political career.

==Political career==
Tanizaki was first elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in 2011, representing the Hirogawa 1st district. He quickly gained a reputation as a fiscal conservative and an advocate for market-oriented reforms. He held several key positions within the LDP, including Minister of Finance and Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry, where he was known for his efforts to reduce government spending and promote free trade agreements.

On June 3, 2016, Tanizaki led an internal party "coup" against Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], who was plagued by the [[Paraña Bank Scandal]] and historic low approval ratings. Forcing Kurosawa's resignation, Tanizaki was elected as the new LDP president by the party caucus and was subsequently sworn in as Prime Minister.

==Prime Minister==
Tanizaki's tenure as Prime Minister was one of the shortest in Okaiken's history, lasting only 54 days. His brief premiership was widely seen as a transitional period for the LDP, which was struggling with internal divisions and declining public support. His administration's main focus was to stabilize the government ahead of the upcoming snap election.

During this time, the [[Communist Party of Okaiken]], led by the charismatic [[Sho Aoyagi]], was gaining significant momentum. In the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|July 2016 snap election]], the LDP suffered a historic defeat, losing 156 seats and falling to third place behind the Communist Party and the Progressive Party.

Following this catastrophic defeat, Tanizaki resigned as both Prime Minister and LDP president on July 26, 2016, the day after the election. He also vacated his Diet seat for the Hirogawa 1st district, which was subsequently won by Keito Maeda of the Progressive Party in a by-election.

==Post-Premiership and Political Comeback==
After his 2016 resignation, Tanizaki stepped back from frontline politics but remained an influential figure within the LDP, working to rebuild his conservative faction.

In 2019, he staged a political comeback, challenging the interim LDP leader, Kenjiro Hino. Arguing that the party had lost its fiscal conservative moorings, Tanizaki won the leadership election. Shortly thereafter, the LDP-affiliated representative serving in his old Hirogawa 1st district resigned, triggering a by-election. Tanizaki won the by-election, returning to the House of Commoners.

Tanizaki led the LDP into the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]]. However, his campaign was seen as disorganized and backward-looking, hampered by party infighting and a failed attempt to link PPO leader [[Honda Shigetsuma]] to the old Paraña Bank Scandal. The LDP failed to gain traction, stagnating with a loss of 4 seats. In the face of this second electoral failure, Tanizaki announced his second resignation as party leader.

==Later career==
Tanizaki remained in the Diet as a backbencher. Under new, more moderate leadership, the LDP saw a major resurgence in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 election]], gaining 40 seats under [[Koichiro Sadanuma]]. In a stunning political upset, however, Tanizaki was one of the rare high-profile LDP members to be defeated. He lost his Hirogawa 1st district seat to a PPO challenger, his personal unpopularity seen as a drag that even the national pro-LDP swing could not overcome.

==Personal life==
[[Sadakazu Tanizaki]] is married to Kyoko Yamaguchi, whom he wed in 2010. They have two children, Yuujin and Yuma.

